  "name": "Shirat nashim (Yemenite Jewish women's song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Judeo-Arabic songs of Yemenite Jewish women, who were not taught the Hebrew liturgy of the men and kept their own oral repertoire for the henna night before a wedding, the wedding itself, births and circumcisions and daily work. A woman singer or a pair of singers leads songs on love, the bride and groom, family and current events while beating a tin can or a copper tray, which the credited auxiliary percussion card stands for. The practice continued among Yemenite Jews in Israel after the 1949-50 migration.",
